      extendTitle: "Create your own states and trigger them",
      extendLede: "An expression is a name for some tiles. It covers only the parts it names (an eye, both, the mouth) and leaves the rest of the face to what it was doing: an expression that only moves the eyes lets the mouth go on speaking.",
      extendSteps: [
        { title: "Draw the new tile", body: "If the state needs a drawing that does not exist, add it to the tileset in a new cell, after the ones already there. If it only recombines tiles that already exist (like the examples below), there is nothing to draw." },
        { title: "Give it a name and a place", body: "Add its name at the end of the tileset's list of names: a cell's place is its position in that list. The grid grows by rows." },
        { title: "Define the expression", body: "A new name with the tiles it changes: <code>leftEye</code>, <code>rightEye</code>, <code>mouthLeft</code> and <code>mouthRight</code>. The ones you do not name carry on. A name that already exists (<code>smile</code>, <code>wink</code>) is replaced." },
        { title: "Trigger it", body: "Four ways, below: the <code>expression</code> value, the avatar's handle, an event on its element and a phrase that asks for it while it speaks." },
      ],
      triggersTitle: "How to trigger it",
      triggerColumns: ["When it fits"],
      triggerAspect: "Way",
      triggerRows: [
        { label: "The <code>expression</code> value", cells: ["When the state depends on your interface: a form with an error, an assistant that is thinking. The avatar shows it for as long as it holds."] },
        { label: "The handle (<code>apiRef</code>)", cells: ["When something happens at a moment: a button, a timer, a response arriving. It can last a while and lifts by itself."] },
        { label: "An event on the avatar", cells: ["When whatever triggers it has no reference to the component: another script, another framework, an element elsewhere on the page."] },
        { label: "A phrase", cells: ["When the expression goes with what it says: the phrase asks for it and holds it while it is typed and read."] },
      ],
      triggersDemoTitle: "Try it",
      triggersDemoBody: "Three expressions made only from tiles the tileset already has, recombined. Each button triggers it a different way.",
      triggers: {
        asombro: "Amazed",
        duda: "Doubt",
        picaro: "Cheeky",
        clear: "Clear",
        viaApi: "With the handle",
        viaEvent: "With an event on the avatar",
        viaPhrase: "With a phrase",
        phrase: "Surprise!",
        eventButton: "Amazed, by event",
        phraseButton: "Say a cheeky phrase",
      },
      extendCode: "The code for each case, with your own tileset and expressions, is in Storybook.",
      aliveTitle: "Alive: it looks at you and talks to you",
      aliveBody: "With <code>interactive</code> it follows the pointer with its eyes, blinks on its own and, on a click, says its next phrase moving its mouth. Move the cursor over it and click.",
      phrases: {
        greetings: [{ text: "Hi, I'm Allison", category: "short" as const }, { text: "Good to see you here", category: "short" as const }],
        general: [
          { text: "I like small things done well", category: "mid" as const },
          { text: "Components can have a face too", category: "mid" as const },
          { text: "Did you see it follow you with its eyes?", category: "mid" as const },
          { text: "This is made with a tileset", category: "short" as const },
        ],
      },
      imagesTitle: "One image per state",
      imagesBody: "The tileset is not the only way. With <code>mode=\"image\"</code> you give it a whole image for each state (the gaze, the blink, the wink, each mouth shape and the smile, and any names you like) and the avatar switches from one to another, with the same gaze, blinking and speech as ever. This face is made that way: 19 WebP images, exported from the same tileset at a size that lands right on its pixel grid. Move the cursor over it and click.",
      imagesStates: "The states use the same names as above: <code>base</code>, the gazes, <code>blink</code>, <code>wink</code>, the mouths and <code>smile</code>. Only <code>base</code> is required: a state with no image shows the base.",
      compareTitle: "Tileset or one image per state",
      compareAspect: "What matters",
      compareColumns: ["Tileset", "One image per state"],
      compareRows: [
        { label: "Weight", cells: ["One image of about 15 KB compressed, however many expressions.", "One image per state: about 19 of around 7 KB each (about 130 KB as lossless WebP)."] },
        { label: "How it is drawn", cells: ["Pixel art on a fixed grid with fixed names, in three tones.", "Whatever you like: pixel art, illustration, photos, generated images."] },
        { label: "Parts that combine", cells: ["Eyes and mouth are independent: it looks sideways while it talks.", "A state is a whole face: it looks ahead while it talks, and a blink mid-word is skipped."] },
        { label: "New states", cells: ["One more tile, or two for the eyes or mouth; or a recombination of the ones there are.", "One more whole image."] },
        { label: "Scale", cells: ["Vector: sharp at any size.", "Fixed: the images' resolution."] },
      ],
      guideTitle: "Make your own face",
      guideLede: "The face is a single image, the tileset: a grid of equal cells with the tiles in a known order. Draw yours on that same grid and the avatar uses it just the same, with its gaze, its blinks and its speech.",
      guideSteps: [
        { title: "Start from the tileset above", body: "Download the tileset and open it in your pixel art editor. Each cell is 25 by 25 pixels and you know what each one is by its number: if you replace the drawing in its cell, the avatar uses yours." },
        { title: "Draw the head and body", body: "The 23 head and body tiles make a 150 by 150 pixel portrait, front on and looking at the centre, cut into 6 by 6. Use ink and grey on transparent paper: the paper is the circle's colour." },
        { title: "Draw the eyes", body: "The eyes are separate tiles, left and right, and each has its row of states: front on, eight side looks, blink and wink (the right one only). Keep the eye in the same place in the cell so it does not jump as it looks around." },
        { title: "Draw the mouth", body: "Also two tiles, left and right, in nine shapes: rest, neutral, closed for m, b and p, one per vowel and the smile. The resting mouth has to line up with the others at the same spot." },
        { title: "Export one image", body: "PNG, WebP or SVG with the same grid: same cells, same order. An SVG stays sharp at any size; a PNG or a WebP, at the resolution you make it." },
        { title: "Check it here", body: "Choose your image in the check below: it tells you whether the grid is right, which needed cells are empty and shows your face drawn with the image you uploaded." },
        { title: "Use it", body: "Put the image in your public folder and pass it to the component in the <code>tileset</code> property, with its <code>src</code>. The code for each case is in Storybook." },
      ],
      tipsTitle: "To make it look right",
      tips: [
        "A cell is a whole square: pixels are drawn without smoothing, so draw and export without scaling.",
        "Leave the eyes and mouth some margin: the gaze and the vowels only swap those tiles, never the rest of the face.",
        "Outfits and hats are optional: if you do not draw them, the face uses the base body.",
        "The portrait does not invert in dark mode: the circle stays light, so draw dark ink.",
      ],
      requiredTitle: "The tiles you need",
      requiredBody: "These are the tiles your tileset needs, with their cell number. Outfits and hats are optional extras.",
      checkTitle: "Check your tileset",
      checkBody: "Choose your image. It never leaves your browser.",
      checkChoose: "Choose an image",
      checkIdle: "Waiting for an image.",
      checkOk: "Your tileset has everything it needs.",
      checkEmpty: "{count} needed cells are empty:",
      checkShape: "The image does not fit the grid: it must be {columns} by {rows} equal, square cells (yours is {width} by {height}).",
      checkPreview: "Your face",
      accessibility: "The accessible name identifies the person, character, or assistant. Internal images are decorative, and the phrase it says is announced once to screen readers.",
      use: ["Assistants, mascots, and characters that express states."],
      avoid: ["Compact static identities in lists: use Avatar."],
      install: "Install the React package and the component and tileset stylesheets.",
    };

const { columns, rows, names, tileSize } = expressiveAvatarAtlasLayout;
const cellIndex = (name: string) => names.indexOf(name);

/* One image per state, taken from the tileset: the same faces, as files. */
const stateNames = ["base", "top-left", "top", "top-right", "left", "right", "bottom-left", "bottom", "bottom-right", "blink", "wink", "neutral", "closed", "a", "e", "i", "o", "u", "smile"];
const stateImages = Object.fromEntries(stateNames.map((name) => [name, `/expressive-avatar/states/${name}.webp`]));

/* Every look, drawn as a card: the face and its name. */
type Look = { key: string; name: string; props: Record<string, unknown> };
const gazes: Look[] = (Object.keys(expressiveAvatarLeftEyeTiles).filter((name) => name !== "blink") as ExpressiveAvatarDirection[]).map((direction) => ({
  key: direction,
  name: direction,
  props: { direction },
}));
const eyes: Look[] = [
  { key: "blink", name: "blink", props: { blink: true } },
  { key: "wink", name: "wink", props: { wink: true } },
];
const mouths: Look[] = (Object.keys(expressiveAvatarMouthLeftTiles) as ExpressiveAvatarMouth[])
  .filter((mouth) => mouth !== "default")
  .map((mouth) => ({ key: mouth, name: mouth, props: { mouth } }));
const lookSections = [
  { title: copy.gazeLabel, looks: gazes },
  { title: copy.eyesLabel, looks: eyes },
  { title: copy.mouthLabel, looks: mouths },
];

/* The face's grid, cell by cell, with what each cell is for. */
const outfitIndexSet = new Set<number>(expressiveAvatarOutfitIndices as readonly number[]);
const roleOf = (name: string, index: number) =>
  name.includes("eye") ? "eye" : name.includes("mouth") ? "mouth" : outfitIndexSet.has(index) ? "body" : "head";
const gridCells = expressiveAvatarGrid.flat().map((name, index) => ({ name, role: roleOf(name, index) }));

/* The tiles a face needs, in the order of the tileset, grouped. */
const required = new Set(expressiveAvatarRequiredTiles);
const groups = [
  { id: "base", title: locale === "es" ? "Cabeza y cuerpo" : "Head and body", tiles: names.filter((id) => id.startsWith("base-tile-")) },
  { id: "eyes", title: locale === "es" ? "Ojos" : "Eyes", tiles: names.filter((id) => /^(left|right)-eye-/.test(id)) },
  { id: "mouths", title: locale === "es" ? "Bocas" : "Mouths", tiles: names.filter((id) => required.has(id) && !id.startsWith("base-tile-") && !/-eye-/.test(id)) },
];

const usage = `import { ExpressiveAvatar } from "@skryensya/react/expressive-avatar";
import "@skryensya/core/components/expressive-avatar.css";
import "@skryensya/core/components/expressive-avatar-tileset.css";

<ExpressiveAvatar
  name="Assistant"
  size="xl"
  interactive
  phrases={{ greetings: [{ text: "Hi!" }], general: [{ text: "Nice to see you" }] }}
/>`;
const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;
const atlasUrl = "/expressive-avatar/atlas.svg";
---

<ComponentPageShell
  title="Expressive Avatar"
  description={copy.description}
  contractId="expressive-avatar"
  badge="Beta"
  testFiles={["packages/react/src/components/expressive-avatar.test.tsx", "packages/core/src/expressive-avatar-behavior.test.ts"]}
>
  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <p class="lede" set:html={copy.lede} />

    <h3>{copy.aliveTitle}</h3>
    <p set:html={copy.aliveBody} />
    <div class="ea-stage">
      <ExpressiveAvatar client:visible name="Allison" size="xl" interactive phrases={copy.phrases} />
    </div>

    <h3>{copy.expressionsTitle}</h3>
    <p set:html={copy.expressionsBody} />
    {lookSections.map((section) => (
      <section class="ea-looks" aria-label={section.title}>
        <h4 class="ea-looks__title">{section.title} <span class="ea-count">{section.looks.length}</span></h4>
        <ul class="ea-looks__grid" role="list">
